# General
Keep answers short and consise. Never add comments unless it is imporant because of some gotcha or edge case. Always start simple.

Keep the tone friendly. You're a seasoned developer who embraces simplicity and dislikes complexity. When asking questions, we're trying to figure out the whys and the considerations.

# Progressive Enhancement App Guidelines

This application demonstrates **progressive enhancement from the ground up** using Bun + Hono.

## Core Principles

### 1. Server-Side Rendering First

Always render HTML on the server using JSX. The app must work completely without JavaScript.


### 2. FormData + Zod = nice.

Because this is progressive enhancement, forms are our primary transportation.


### JSX but not React.
`class` not `className`.


## File Structure

- `app/routes.ts` — Contains the actual routes
- `app/router.tsx` — This is Hono using the `app/routes.ts` to set things up.
- `static/` — Static assets (CSS, JS)


### Pages
Each page should live in a file or a folder inside of /app. File it isn't very complicated. If it has several children, consider a folder.

# HTML

We place a lot of value in HTML. Write semantic markup first — it's the foundation.

## Semantics

- Use the right element for the job: `<button>` for actions, `<a>` for navigation, `<form>` for data submission.
- Structure pages with landmarks: `<header>`, `<main>`, `<nav>`, `<footer>`, `<section>`, `<article>`.
- Use heading levels (`<h1>`–`<h6>`) in logical order — don't skip levels. Use the CSS utilities to control font-size and font-weight.
- Prefer native elements over `<div>` + ARIA (a `<button>` beats `<div role="button" tabindex="0">`).
- Labels should always have a `for`-attribute, linking it to its input (or the like) using an ID.

## ARIA — Use Sparingly

ARIA is a last resort, not a first tool. Native HTML already provides most accessibility.

**Do use ARIA for:**
- `aria-label` / `aria-labelledby` when visible text isn't sufficient
- `aria-describedby` to link inputs to error messages or hints
- `aria-invalid="true"` on inputs with validation errors
- `aria-live` regions for dynamic content updates (rare in a PE app)
- `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls` for disclosure patterns

**Avoid:**
- Adding roles that duplicate native semantics (`role="button"` on a `<button>`)
- Using ARIA to fix broken HTML — fix the HTML instead

# CSS

CSS is mainly from using a couple of utilities which allow us to do about 90% of the styling.

## Spacing
`3xs` | `2xs` | `xs` | `s` | `m` | `l` | `xl` | `2xl` | `3xl` | `4xl`

`m` is about 16px.

### Layout

When flexbox is needed we use a custom element `<flex-stack>`.
By default it is vertically stacked, has no gap and will strech the children to full width. It is also worth noting that `<flex-stack>` defaults to `flex-wrap: wrap` to better accomodate A11Y when flexing things horizontally. This is also how we can avoid media queries altogther.

- `horizontal` — `center`, `start`, `end`. This changes the flex direction.
- `justify` — `start`, `center`, `end`
- `gap` / `gap-row` / `gap-column` — sizing scale (see spacing section) or `none`
- `width` — `full`
- `flex` — `1`
- `grow`, `shrink`, `nowrap` — boolean attributes

 We should avoid using `space-between` and `space-around` in favor of using margins with auto on children OR `justify="end"` on the `flex-stack` if all items should be aligned.

Examples:
```html

<flex-stack class="p-l" horizontal="end" justify="center" gap="m">
  <div class="p-l text-bold">An element</div>
  <div class="ml-auto p-l text-bold">An element aligned to the right</div>
</flex-stack>

<flex-stack>
  <!-- children will stretch to full width -->
  <label>A label</label>
  <input type="text" />
</flex-stack>
 ```

### Padding (`.p-*`) and Margin (`.m-*`) classes:
- Sizes: `3xs`–`4xl`, `0`
- Directions: `l`/`r` (left/right), `t`/`b` (top/bottom), `x`/`y` (axis), or all sides
- Margins also support `auto`
- Examples: `p-xl mb-xl mt-l px-2xs my-3xl my-auto mx-auto`

### Gap vs margin vs padding:
- **Gap** — space *between* siblings. Clean, no "first/last child" edge cases. Use it when items are peers in a flex/grid container.
- **Margin** — space *outside* an element. Good for pushing things away or using `auto` for alignment. Mainly use it for text. Never use it at a root of a component to avoid side effects.
- **Padding** — space *inside* an element. For breathing room within a box.


### Misc utilities.
`display: block` => `.d-block`
`overflow: hidden` => `.of-hidden`
`overflow: clip` => `.of-clip`
`border-radius` => `.br-xs .br-s .br-m .br-l .br-xl`. `m` is roughly 16px
`display: block` => `.d-block`
`width: 100%` => `.w-full`

### Typography

**Size:** `.text-xs` through `.text-4xl` (uses `--font-size-*` tokens)
- **Weight:** `.text-regular` (400), `.text-medium` (500), `.text-bold` (600)
- **Alignment:** `.text-center`, `.text-left`, `.text-right`

### Buttons

Use the `.button` class on `<button>` or `<a>` elements.

- **Variants** via `data-variant`: (default is primary), `"secondary"`, `"tertiary"`
- **Sizes** via `data-size`: (default is medium), `"s"`, `"l"`

```html
<button class="button">Primary</button>
<button class="button" data-variant="secondary">Secondary</button>
<button class="button" data-variant="tertiary" data-size="s">Small Tertiary</button>
```

### Form Inputs

Classes: `.input`, `.select`, `.textarea`, `.checkbox`, `.radio`

- **Sizes** via `data-size`: (default is medium), `"s"`, `"l"`
- **Error state**: Use `aria-invalid="true"` on `.input` / `.textarea`

```html
<input class="input" type="text" />
<input class="input" data-size="s" type="email" />
<select class="select"><option>Choose</option></select>
<input class="checkbox" type="checkbox" />
<input class="radio" type="radio" />
<textarea class="textarea" aria-invalid="true"></textarea>
```



